A||||16||Gold-86SE||Tinting Your Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||05-09-2001||02:29 PM||||<BR>The summer sun is coming on down here is Tejas and I am thinking about having my 84 tinted to help fight the uv rays.<P>I am curious what those of you who had your Fiero tinted did, was it self-installed or shop-intalled?<P>Pros, Cons, and cost will help me determine what I am going to do.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Not So Fair Weather Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-09-2001||02:36 PM||||One con is some states (like mine) have banned tinted glass - too many cops have been taken by surprize when they walked up to cars they couldnt see inside.<P>  also a safety issue - really restricts your night vision.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||05-09-2001||02:45 PM||||I have seen tinting in the prototype phase that is like a crizal(??) transistions lense , the more sunlight it recieves , the darker it gets to a certian degree..The less sunlight , the lighter..<P>I will get more info on it if anyone is interested<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000003||JSocha||05-09-2001||03:27 PM||||Ken:  Have always tinted my windows (w/5% grade - back and side windows - even though it is illegal here in ND to be on the drivers/passenger windows) with tint from Madico (got somebody to get me some) and I tell you I have never had a problem with seeing at night except out the rear window through the mirror (same problem even without window tint).  Had a cop ride with me one dark night and he himself was surprised how well he could see out the side for how dark it was from what the state gov had told them.  He himself never bothered me again after that but that is because I live in a small town.  Have to have good quality tint though.  Not here to razz you Ken, just my experience.<P>Slam:  I seen those windows too on a vehicle in Grand Forks.  Way to cool!  Never could find any info on it, who it was or where he had it done and I would be interested.<P>I heard somebody had custom glass made where they had the "photo chemical" layered within their windshield.  That would have been awsome to see if it was true to begin with.<P>Gold:  You could have it shop-installed, however, they go just below the door trim weather seal on the inside, which if not done properly, will catch and tear (and in my past experience as well as seeing typically starts to lift there overtime).  <P>Also, they typically leave to much of a "margin edge" around your glass that you can see from the inside when your window is closed.<P>I've always done my own, by removing both the drivers/passenger glass, laying it on a towel on a table, tinting it way down towards the bottom (where it is not seen) and then using clear finger nail polish/coating on the unseen "lip" of the window tint to the glass to insure it would never lift, including around the window edge it self where I left less then a 1/8" edge margin all around (just enough for supposed expansion/contraction).<P>If you need pointers, email me as I have done lots and lots and lots and lots of window tinting for friends, family as well as myself.  <P>Very few "seeable" bubbles (unless you get really, really close to the glass), long lasting and short cuts and pointers.<P>I want to do my '87GT this winter only if I can get the body shop to repaint my vehicle this winter and remove the DD/PD glass for me to take home.  Otherwise, somebody out here on this forum I hope will give me pointers on how to remove this glass without tearing into the interior door panel and paper.<P>Also going to do my 2001 Dodge one ton; my wife's 1997 Dodge 1500; my friends 2000 Ford F150; and another friends 1967(?) Camero (he has been after me for years but he wants me to buy the window tint).<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroLisa||05-09-2001||03:36 PM||||I have had 2 cars with tinted windows- my Fiero had 20% all around and I got a ticket for $50 for it...kinda ticked me off. But I'm still gonna do the GT.  In Ohio the legal limit is 50% which is just about worthless, its so light.<P>I have done it myself and had it professionally done. If you have a lot of time and patience, I suggest doing it yourself, but it takes a couple tries to get the technique down. The rear window is also a big pain i the butt.<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 violet pearl 4 speed GT: 17 in. rims, lowered, cold-air intake<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A><P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>Tarheels Fieros Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||05-09-2001||04:51 PM||||Get it done in a shop. It's a big hassle todo it right, and if you are a novice at it you'll spend more money om materials that you screw up then it would cost to have it done.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||05-09-2001||07:22 PM||||my '87 is the only car i was able to do all the windows on, since they're all fairly flat. i can see flaws but it has the "did it myself" factor going for it. 5% all around with band on the top and bottom of the winshield. one roll's not a huge investment. get a roll and try a window or see if a friend had some left from their install, as long as it's a brand you can get more of yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000007||PBJ||05-09-2001||07:38 PM||||Tinting is like anything else. If you want a job to last, you're going to need to get it done professionally (unless you personally know someone who does it).  Tint film comes in different qualities.  Cheap stuff goes purple easy.  I've had my cars in the past tinted and paid the money for it.  It also bubbles less and there is less chance of peeling.  Tint is suppose to make your car look good as well as cooling the car.  If not done right, you car will look like crap.  If you want to do it yourself, practice on someone else's car!||||reg||1
Z||000008||saleenfiero||05-09-2001||07:53 PM||||I tried it on my car last year.  Was super hard to get looking actually right.  ESPECIALLY that damn back window.  It would probably be alot easier without the window in the car or without the seats inside.<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||6
Z||000009||operator64||05-09-2001||08:03 PM||||   If you do decide to do it yourself buy <BR>3-M brand tint, you will have to get this at a shop that installs tint but from my experiences they will sell it to you.<BR>   Make sure your glass is very very clean this is one of the big deals to making the job turn out good ( along with a lot of patience ). good luck in whatever you decide to do.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sjp777||05-09-2001||08:40 PM||||It only cost me $60 to have both door windows done professionally and with a lifetime warranty. <P>Here's a tip: if you leave the rear window untinted (don't really need it) you can get away with much darker side windows because more light is let in and cops behind you don't see any tint at all. If you do get pulled over, just roll down both windows as the officer approaches the car. They don't like tinted windows because they can't see potential danger as they approach the car. If you eliminate their inabilty to see, they are usually less likely to issue tickets.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||MoeDrivin||05-09-2001||09:57 PM||||Good call sjp777!  Thanks for the tip--I'll remember that when the state troopers are trying to meet their quota for the day.<P>Who knew tinting ticked them off...<p>[This message has been edited by MoeDrivin (edited 05-09-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000012||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-10-2001||01:25 AM||||They sell different grades of tint at the auto parts chains now.<P>I bought some that was gaurantied not to fade.  It was about $15 for enough to do 2.5 back windows.  This was my third time tinting and it turned out well.  From the outside, up close, there are two small bubbles at the edge of the tint.  One is near the center at the top and the second is at the passenger side top corner.   <P>From inside the car, it looks perfect.  The tint seems to be holding up well, but it will take a few summers to be certain.<P>It was much easier than tinting curved glass.  You can do it in one piece.  <P>Go for it, life is short!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Galen||05-10-2001||01:37 AM||||From what I've heard cops can't pull you for a back window being  tinted. My7? any words on this?<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||87GTjoe||05-10-2001||08:21 AM||||I cant speak for other states but as far as South Carolina is concerned as long as you have a mirror on the passenger side of the vehicle you can black out your rear window.||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroLisa||05-10-2001||09:50 AM||||Galen- since they can't test your back or front window, they can't do much about it...and anyway in most places its not illegal for back (but is for front). The device they use to check it slides over the top of your window, so your window has to be partially down.<P>About half of every occaision when a cop has pulled me over, he has checked my window tint and given me a ticket for it... GRR (OK so thats only 2 tickets but still..)<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 violet pearl 4 speed GT: 17 in. rims, lowered, cold-air intake<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>Tarheels Fieros Website</A><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/daytona1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Spektyr||05-10-2001||11:11 AM||||I haven't gotten my Fiero tinted (yet) but my mild street Nissan PU was.  Different states have different laws, just ask your local window tinting shop what they are.  Those guys know them by heart.<P>My advice:  Get it done professionally.  I did on the Nissan and people thought I had smoked glass, not tinting film.  There were NO imperfections anywhere, and the back window of a pickup is a lot like the Fiero's.  Also, get the nicest tint you can afford.  The more UV protection you get the happier you'll be.  UV is what kills the interior, and builds most of the heat.<P>My Nissan was almost legal for Kansas when I moved back here.  The neat little trick I did was having the rear window tinted darker than the sides.  The side windows were legal by 2%, but the back window was over by about 15%.  I never got tested by a cop, but they would've been dissapointed.  The side windows (which they can test) would pass, but the back window (which they can't test) wouldn't.<P>Sure, it looks neat to have those super-dark tinted windows, but I prefer the lighter tint for several reasons.<P>1.  If it's really hot, really cold, or raining and I get pulled over, I don't want to sit there with the windows all the way down while the cop harrasses me just so I can avoid the tint ticket.<BR>2.  I like it when a police officer approaches my car WITHOUT his hand on his gun.  Call my fickle.<BR>3.  I'd rather a stereo thief look into my car, see that the stereo isn't worth stealing, and go to someone elses car.  I mean c'mon, the Fiero is tiny inside, I don't need the 190 decible stereo system.<BR>4.  Even 38% tint cuts headlights enough that I'm not blinded by them.  Then again, I've also learned not to stare into them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
